We built an autonomous platform that generates interactive web tools without any humans writing code. But we kept hitting two major problems. The first was lost intent. An AI agent would build a tool and make a deliberate design choice, and then weeks later, another agent would try to improve it and accidentally undo that choice because the reasoning was never recorded. The second problem was that just because a tool deployed successfully didn't mean it actually worked. An agent could write perfectly valid HTML, passing all structural checks, but the tool would still be broken in the browser—like a slider mapped to the wrong variable or a chart drawn on the wrong axis.  To fix this, we forced the tools to carry their own documentation. Whenever an agent creates a tool, it writes a PLAN and a NOTES stream, saving them directly to a Postgres database keyed to the tool itself. The PLAN defines exactly what the tool is supposed to do, logs deliberate design decisions, and lists strict, machine-readable acceptance criteria. The NOTES stream is an append-only history of every fix, diagnosis, and dead end. Recording dead ends is crucial because it stops the next repair agent from wasting time on a fix that already failed.  Of course, acceptance criteria are useless unless something actually tests them. We run checks on a tiered ladder. It starts with free structural checks to make sure the code exists, moves to cheap static checks that verify HTML containers are present, and ends with an expensive behavioural tier. In that final tier, headless Chromium opens the live page on desktop and mobile profiles, interacts with the tool, and proves it actually works. We enforce an "anchor rule" for the cheap static checks, meaning they only look for the outermost container. This stops the tests from failing on dynamic content generated by the browser at runtime, and it catches the AI when it hallucinates fake CSS selectors.  The entire maintenance loop runs on a schedule without anyone pressing a button. A sweep finds tools due for a check, the headless browser tries them, and the verdict is written straight into the tool's notes. If a test fails, the browser photographs the live broken page and files a ticket. Importantly, the system works out who is to blame before assigning the fix. In one instance, a mobile quiz page overflowed the screen. The system measured the containers, realized the tool was fine, and identified that a shared site footer template was actually causing the overflow. It routed the fix to the site template instead of raising an unfixable ticket against the quiz tool.  Under the hood, it uses ordinary parts: Postgres for truth, Kafka for routing, a Go chassis for workflows, and Claude models for writing and reasoning. We just made sure that a failure to write documentation never crashes the actual build. Running this system in production between July 4 and 16, 2026, taught us an interesting lesson. The real value wasn't just tools fixing themselves. It was that forcing the machine to write down its beliefs exposed exactly when it was wrong, highlighting invented selectors and phantom delivery mechanisms that we otherwise would have missed.

